{
  "dom_id": "step-q342885",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200385/assets/1505bc3714405515ec7d5a834c6d08f849b3adbf8678a76b3b5cf27ccf394e28.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-3,3]$ as shown above. In **ascending order**, the points of inflection of  $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in (-3,3)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x=$\n\n{{field-2}}, $\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-3,3].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{″}$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-3,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the table, we see that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign at the points $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=2$ (note that ${f}^{″}(x)$ does not change the sign when it passes through $x=-1$ and $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the points of inflection are \n\n$$\nx=-2,\\,x=0,\\,x=2.\n$$",
